      "settings": {
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/portrait-artist.jpg",
        "heading": "My story",
        "lead": "<p>I'm Andrea Bowman, an abstract painter working from a studio in Cape Town. I paint large, layered canvases, and I make sculptural pots for the home and garden.</p>",
        "text": "<p>I trained at the Metafora Art School in Barcelona, and my paintings have since appeared in five magazines, chosen by leading interior designers for the homes they create. Travel feeds everything I make: the light, colour and texture of places like Oman come home with me as photographs, and find their way into paint and concrete.</p>"
      }
    },
    "how_i_work": {
      "type": "narrow-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A painting usually begins with a place rather than a picture: a coastline, a weathered wall, the light at the end of a winter afternoon. Once I start, I find it very hard to stop. The studio fills up, days pass, and only the moment counts until the piece is where it needs to be.</p><p>I build each surface slowly, in acrylic, oil, charcoal and mixed media, scraping back and layering again so the edges of earlier layers show through. Every painting carries its own history.</p>"
          }
        },
        "quote_1": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "I love what I make, then I can't live with it, and a month later I love it all over again."
          }
        },
        "text_2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A painting is finished when I stop wanting to change it. Then the quieter work begins: framing, finishing touches and the people who help each piece reach the home it belongs in.</p><p>The same pull goes into my pots. After a trip to Oman I had to make the huge Omani pots I had in mind. Each one took five men to move into place, stands nearly as tall as I am, and will outlive me.</p><p>I am ambitious about my work, and I know there is always more to learn. That is what keeps me going back to the studio.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
